\section{Runtime Support for Parallelism}\label{sec:parallelism}

\subsection{Work Stealing}\label{sec:work-stealing}

\begin{definition}[Work Stealing]\label{def:work-stealing}
\emph{Work stealing} is a scheduling strategy where each worker thread maintains a local deque of tasks. When a worker's deque is empty, it ``steals'' tasks from another worker's deque (typically from the bottom, to minimize contention).
\end{definition}

Work stealing provides:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Load balancing}: work is automatically distributed across workers.
  \item \textbf{Cache locality}: tasks created by a worker are likely to be executed by the same worker.
  \item \textbf{Low contention}: workers mostly operate on their own deques without synchronization.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Task Parallelism}\label{sec:task-parallelism}

The runtime may support task-based parallelism:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Fork-join}: spawn parallel tasks and wait for all to complete.
  \item \textbf{Dataflow tasks}: tasks with explicit data dependencies, scheduled when inputs are ready.
  \item \textbf{Continuation-based}: tasks are represented as continuations, scheduled by the runtime.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Compiler Support for Parallelism}\label{sec:compiler-parallelism}

The compiler supports parallelism through:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Auto-parallelization}: automatically converting sequential loops into parallel ones.
  \item \textbf{OpenMP support}: recognizing pragmas and generating parallel code.
  \item \textbf{Vectorization}: converting scalar loops into SIMD instructions (\cref{sec:vectorization}).
  \item \textbf{Thread-safety analysis}: checking that shared data is properly synchronized.
\end{itemize}

\section{Concurrency and the Type System}\label{sec:concurrency-types}

\subsection{Type-Based Concurrency Safety}\label{sec:type-concurrency}

Some languages use the type system to prevent concurrency bugs:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Rust}: \texttt{Send} and \texttt{Sync} traits ensure that types are safe to share across threads.
  \item \textbf{Linear types}: ensure that resources are used exactly once, preventing data races.
  \item \textbf{Session types}: ensure that communication protocols are followed correctly.
  \item \textbf{Ownership types}: track which thread owns which data.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Actor Model}\label{sec:actor-model}

\begin{definition}[Actor Model]\label{def:actor-model}
The \emph{actor model} is a concurrency model where computation is performed by \emph{actors} that communicate exclusively through asynchronous message passing. Each actor processes messages sequentially, eliminating shared state.
\end{definition}

The actor model is used in Erlang, Akka (Scala), and Orleans (.NET). It eliminates data races by construction but requires careful protocol design to avoid deadlocks and livelocks.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:concurrency-summary}

Concurrency support is a critical component of any modern language runtime. From low-level OS threads and atomic operations to high-level async/await and green threads, the runtime must provide a spectrum of concurrency mechanisms that balance performance, programmability, and safety. The memory model---the formal specification of how concurrent memory accesses behave---is the theoretical foundation that makes all of this possible.

\begin{historical}
Concurrency in programming languages has evolved from the raw threads of the 1980s (pthreads, Win32 threads) through the actor model of Erlang (1986), the Java memory model (2004), to the modern async/await pattern popularized by C\# (2012) and adopted by Rust, Python, JavaScript, and others. Go's goroutines (2009) demonstrated that lightweight threading could be practical at massive scale. The C11/C++11 memory model (2011) provided the first widely-adopted formal specification of concurrent memory semantics, resolving years of debate about what concurrent programs mean. The evolution continues with effect systems, structured concurrency, and verified concurrent algorithms pushing the boundaries of what is achievable.
\end{historical}
